// The standard library pages
// ==========================
// The `hive.*` half of what `hive agents` writes into `.hivedocs/`.
//
// A module is split when its parts are reached for separately: somebody writing a
// WebSocket server has no use for the TCP calls, so `net` and `crypto` are several
// pages each. `conv` and `time` are small and whole.
import ./page

// Every page about the standard library, bar `hive.ui`.
func pages(): page.Page[dyn] {
	return [
		page.Page("stdlib/README.md", index()),
		page.Page("stdlib/conv.md", conv()),
		page.Page("stdlib/math.md", math()),
		page.Page("stdlib/map.md", maps()),
		page.Page("stdlib/file.md", files()),
		page.Page("stdlib/term.md", term()),
		page.Page("stdlib/term-exec.md", termExec()),
		page.Page("stdlib/env.md", env()),
		page.Page("stdlib/task.md", task()),
		page.Page("stdlib/time.md", times()),
		page.Page("stdlib/json.md", json()),
		page.Page("stdlib/crypto.md", crypto()),
		page.Page("stdlib/crypto-encrypt.md", cryptoEncrypt()),
		page.Page("stdlib/crypto-jwt.md", cryptoJwt()),
		page.Page("stdlib/net.md", net()),
		page.Page("stdlib/net-http.md", netHttp()),
		page.Page("stdlib/net-websockets.md", netWebsockets()),
		page.Page("stdlib/net-sockets.md", netSockets()),
		page.Page("stdlib/net-names.md", netNames()),
		page.Page("stdlib/net-urls.md", netUrls()),
		page.Page("stdlib/sql.md", sql()),
		page.Page("stdlib/syslink.md", syslink()),
		page.Page("stdlib/syslink-calls.md", syslinkCalls()),
		page.Page("stdlib/syslink-nodes.md", syslinkNodes()),
		page.Page("stdlib/audio.md", audio())
	]
}

// --- The index ---------------------------------------------------------------

func index(): Str {
	return page.backticked(`
		# The standard library

		Reached as ~BT~hive.<module>.<name>~BT~, from anywhere, with no import — or as
		~BT~<module>.<name>~BT~, ~BT~net.httpRequest~BT~, wherever the program has not taken
		that name for something of its own ([modules](../modules.md)).
		~BT~import hive.net as web~BT~ only gives the module another **spelling** —
		~BT~web.httpRequest~BT~ and ~BT~hive.net.httpRequest~BT~ are the same call, and neither
		changes what is linked.

		Each module owns its types under its own namespace: ~BT~hive.net.HttpRequest~BT~,
		~BT~hive.json.JsonError~BT~, ~BT~hive.map.Map~BT~, ~BT~hive.syslink.SyslinkError~BT~. The
		only builtin types that live directly on ~BT~hive~BT~ are the three the language uses
		without a module: ~BT~Result~BT~, ~BT~Table~BT~ and ~BT~Address<M>~BT~.

		**A library call is held to its signature** — how many arguments, and what each
		is — and a type or an enumeration variant the module does not have
		(~BT~hive.net.Nonesuch~BT~, ~BT~hive.ui.TextSize.Small()~BT~) is a compile error rather
		than something the Go toolchain reports later.

		**A failure is a variant.** Where a module can fail in more than one way, its
		error is a union with a variant per way, sharing a ~BT~message~BT~ and whatever else
		the module knows — so it is matched and covered like any union:

		~CODE~hive
		if file.read(path) is Result.Error(file.FileError.NotFound(_, _)) \{
			echo "nothing there yet"
		}
		~CODE~

		These are ~BT~FileError~BT~, ~BT~WsError~BT~, ~BT~SocketError~BT~, ~BT~NetError~BT~, ~BT~SqlError~BT~,
		~BT~CryptoError~BT~, ~BT~SyslinkError~BT~ and the core's ~BT~SecretError~BT~. One comes with the
		module that answers with it, whether or not the program names it, and it prints
		as what went wrong and why.

		**A module you don't use is not in your build.** Every module but two is
		written against the target's standard library alone, so HTTP, WebSockets, TCP,
		JSON, cryptography, spreadsheets and windows all build offline with no
		dependencies. The two exceptions are fetched on the first build that needs them,
		cached, and never linked into a program that does not: ~BT~hive.sql~BT~ links
		database drivers, and ~BT~hive.ui.scene~BT~ is drawn with a pinned three.js.

		| page | module | what is on it |
		| --- | --- | --- |
		| [conv](conv.md) | ~BT~hive.conv~BT~ | rounding, widening, rendering, parsing |
		| [math](math.md) | ~BT~hive.math~BT~ | thirteen ~BT~Float~BT~ functions |
		| [map](map.md) | ~BT~hive.map~BT~ | the dictionary |
		| [file](file.md) | ~BT~hive.file~BT~ | the filesystem |
		| [term](term.md) | ~BT~hive.term~BT~ | stdout, stderr, input, arguments, exit |
		| [term-exec](term-exec.md) | ~BT~hive.term~BT~ | running another program |
		| [env](env.md) | ~BT~hive.env~BT~ | the environment and ~BT~.env~BT~ |
		| [task](task.md) | ~BT~hive.task~BT~ | ~BT~sleep~BT~, ~BT~sleepForever~BT~, and where ~BT~with timeout~BT~ lives |
		| [time](time.md) | ~BT~hive.time~BT~ | Unix milliseconds and ~BT~strftime~BT~ |
		| [json](json.md) | ~BT~hive.json~BT~ | your types *are* the schema |
		| [crypto](crypto.md) | ~BT~hive.crypto~BT~ | hashing, encoding, random |
		| [crypto-encrypt](crypto-encrypt.md) | ~BT~hive.crypto~BT~ | ~BT~encrypt~BT~ / ~BT~decrypt~BT~ |
		| [crypto-jwt](crypto-jwt.md) | ~BT~hive.crypto~BT~ | tokens, as a codec |
		| [net](net.md) | ~BT~hive.net~BT~ | what every server here has in common |
		| [net-http](net-http.md) | ~BT~hive.net~BT~ | HTTP, client and server |
		| [net-websockets](net-websockets.md) | ~BT~hive.net~BT~ | WebSockets |
		| [net-sockets](net-sockets.md) | ~BT~hive.net~BT~ | raw TCP |
		| [net-names](net-names.md) | ~BT~hive.net~BT~ | ~BT~resolve~BT~ and ~BT~localAddress~BT~ |
		| [net-urls](net-urls.md) | ~BT~hive.net~BT~ | ~BT~urlEncode~BT~ and the query string codec |
		| [sql](sql.md) | ~BT~hive.sql~BT~ | connections, drivers, and running a query |
		| [syslink](syslink.md) | ~BT~hive.syslink~BT~ | what a service is |
		| [syslink-calls](syslink-calls.md) | ~BT~hive.syslink~BT~ | every call, and calling an address |
		| [syslink-nodes](syslink-nodes.md) | ~BT~hive.syslink~BT~ | across machines |
		| [ui](ui.md) | ~BT~hive.ui~BT~ | a view is a value — **thirteen pages**, start here |
		| [audio](audio.md) | ~BT~hive.audio~BT~ | a recording or a live stream, by name |

		Reading a table from a CSV or a spreadsheet is [~BT~hive.file~BT~](file.md), and
		the ~BT~Table~BT~ it answers with is [tables](../tables.md). Running a ~BT~query~BT~ is
		[queries](../queries.md).

		*From ~BT~spec/14-stdlib.md~BT~.*
	`)
}

// --- The small, whole modules ------------------------------------------------

func conv(): Str {
	return page.backticked(`
		# ~BT~hive.conv~BT~

		Number and string conversions. Pure, so it works in a ~BT~func~BT~.

		**Hive never widens a number behind your back**, so this module is not a
		convenience — it is the only way an ~BT~Int~BT~ becomes a ~BT~Float~BT~ at all.

		| call | signature |
		| --- | --- |
		| ~BT~ceil(v)~BT~ ~BT~floor(v)~BT~ ~BT~round(v)~BT~ | ~BT~(Float): Int~BT~ |
		| ~BT~itf(v)~BT~ | ~BT~(Int): Float~BT~ |
		| ~BT~itf32(v)~BT~ | ~BT~(Int): Float32~BT~ — the nearest one |
		| ~BT~its(v)~BT~ | ~BT~(Int): Str~BT~ |
		| ~BT~fts(v)~BT~ | ~BT~(Float): Str~BT~ |
		| ~BT~sti(text)~BT~ | ~BT~(Str): Result<Int, ConversionError>~BT~ |
		| ~BT~stf(text)~BT~ | ~BT~(Str): Result<Float, ConversionError>~BT~ |
		| ~BT~bytesOf(text)~BT~ | ~BT~(Str): UInt8[dyn]~BT~ — the bytes it holds |
		| ~BT~fromBytes(bytes)~BT~ | ~BT~(UInt8[]): Str~BT~ — never fails: a ~BT~Str~BT~ holds any bytes |

		~BT~round~BT~ rounds **half away from zero**. A ~BT~ConversionError~BT~ carries the
		offending ~BT~input~BT~ and a short ~BT~message~BT~.

		~CODE~hive
		if hive.conv.sti(field) is Result.Ok(n) \{
			echo n * 2
		}
		echo hive.math.max(hive.conv.itf(0), health)   // ~BT~0~BT~ alone would not compile
		~CODE~

		Converting a ~BT~Float~BT~ that is ~BT~+Inf~BT~, ~BT~-Inf~BT~, ~BT~NaN~BT~ or simply too large to an
		~BT~Int~BT~ is the **one unspecified case in the language** — Go leaves that
		conversion implementation-dependent and Hive does not paper over it. Non-finite
		values reach a program through ~BT~Float~BT~ overflow and through ~BT~stf("Inf")~BT~, so
		check the range you expect before converting if it matters.

		Rendering a value into a string needs none of this:
		~BT~"loaded \{n} of \{total}"~BT~ interpolates anything.
	`)
}

func math(): Str {
	return page.backticked(`
		# ~BT~hive.math~BT~

		| | |
		| --- | --- |
		| **Constants** | ~BT~pi()~BT~ |
		| **Angles** | ~BT~sin~BT~ ~BT~cos~BT~ ~BT~tan~BT~ ~BT~asin~BT~ ~BT~acos~BT~ ~BT~atan2(y, x)~BT~ |
		| **Distances** | ~BT~sqrt~BT~ ~BT~hypot(x, y)~BT~ ~BT~abs~BT~ |
		| **Bounds** | ~BT~min(a, b)~BT~ ~BT~max(a, b)~BT~ ~BT~clamp(value, low, high)~BT~ |

		**Every one takes and answers with a ~BT~Float~BT~, and there is no integer twin.** A
		call handed only ~BT~Float32~BT~s answers a ~BT~Float32~BT~.
		That is one rule for thirteen functions, and it is the same rule the rest of the
		language keeps: ~BT~hive.math.max(0, health)~BT~ is a **compile error** rather than a
		surprise, because ~BT~0~BT~ is an ~BT~Int~BT~. Go in through
		[~BT~hive.conv.itf~BT~](conv.md).

		**Clamping an ~BT~Int~BT~ is an ~BT~if~BT~**, and needs no library.

		Nothing here reports an error, because none of these have one: a value outside a
		function's domain answers with the non-finite ~BT~Float~BT~ the arithmetic already
		produces.

		~BT~atan2~BT~ takes the two legs rather than their ratio, which is what lets it tell
		the four quadrants apart. ~BT~clamp~BT~ is exactly ~BT~min(max(v, low), high)~BT~, so
		bounds the wrong way round answer with ~BT~high~BT~.

		~CODE~hive
		angle := hive.math.atan2(dy, dx)
		reach := hive.math.hypot(dx, dy)
		held  := hive.math.clamp(wanted, 0.0, 1.0)
		~CODE~
	`)
}

func maps(): Str {
	return page.backticked(`
		# ~BT~hive.map~BT~

		A dictionary: keys paired with values, looked up by key. The one collection that
		is not a vector, so it is a module rather than a literal.

		| call | type | what it does |
		| --- | --- | --- |
		| ~BT~new()~BT~ | ~BT~Map<K, T>~BT~ | the empty map |
		| ~BT~set(m, key, value)~BT~ | ~BT~(mut Map<K, T>, K, T): void~BT~ | adds or replaces a pair |
		| ~BT~get(m, key)~BT~ | ~BT~(Map<K, T>, K): Result<T, Bool>~BT~ | the value, or ~BT~Error(false)~BT~ |
		| ~BT~has(m, key)~BT~ | ~BT~(Map<K, T>, K): Bool~BT~ | the same question without the value |
		| ~BT~delete(m, key)~BT~ | ~BT~(mut Map<K, T>, K): void~BT~ | removes a pair |
		| ~BT~keys(m)~BT~ | ~BT~(Map<K, T>): K[dyn]~BT~ | its keys, in order |
		| ~BT~values(m)~BT~ | ~BT~(Map<K, T>): T[dyn]~BT~ | its values, in the same order |
		| ~BT~fromTable(t)~BT~ | ~BT~(Table): Map<Str, Str>~BT~ | reads rows as key/value pairs |
		| ~BT~toTable(m)~BT~ | ~BT~(Map<Str, Str>): Table~BT~ | one two-cell row per pair |

		~CODE~hive
		mut hive.map.Map<Str, Int> counts = hive.map.new()
		hive.map.set(counts, "apple", 3)

		for each key in hive.map.keys(counts) \{
			if hive.map.get(counts, key) is Result.Ok(n) \{
				echo "\{key}: \{n}"
			}
		}
		~CODE~

		## The rules

		* **~BT~new()~BT~ takes its type from where it lands** — a declaration, a ~BT~return~BT~
		  whose callable declares a map, or an argument to a parameter or field declared
		  as one — or, bound with ~BT~:=~BT~, from the **first later use** that says it:
		  ~BT~hive.map.set(m, "a", 1)~BT~ makes ~BT~m~BT~ a ~BT~Map<Str, Int>~BT~ for good, and a later
		  ~BT~set~BT~ of a ~BT~Str~BT~ value is a compile error.
		* **A key is compared and hashed whole**, so it is a ~BT~Str~BT~, ~BT~Int~BT~, ~BT~Float~BT~,
		  ~BT~Bool~BT~ or ~BT~Atom~BT~ — or a declared type whose every field is one of those,
		  which gives a composite key. **Storage cannot be a key**:
		  ~BT~Map<Str[dyn], Int>~BT~ is a compile error.
		* **A map is reached by key, never by position.** ~BT~m[0]~BT~ is a compile error
		  pointing at ~BT~get~BT~, and ~BT~for each~BT~ over a map is one too — a turn of the loop
		  would have to be handed the key or the value, and the syntax never said which.
		  Loop over ~BT~keys(m)~BT~.
		* **The order is the order you set the keys in.** ~BT~keys~BT~, ~BT~values~BT~, ~BT~toTable~BT~
		  and ~BT~echo~BT~ all use it, and replacing a value leaves its key where it was.
		  (Go's own map iterates in a deliberately randomised order; this one prints the
		  same thing twice, which is what makes it testable.)
		* **Only a ~BT~mut~BT~ map can be written to.** ~BT~set~BT~ and ~BT~delete~BT~ follow ~BT~append~BT~'s
		  rule exactly, and a ~BT~proc~BT~ may take a ~BT~m: mut hive.map.Map<K, T>~BT~ parameter.
		* **A map is a value**, and so is what comes out of it: ~BT~keys~BT~ and ~BT~values~BT~ are
		  fresh vectors.
		* **~BT~==~BT~ compares the pairs** and ignores the order they were set in. There is
		  no ordering *between* maps, so ~BT~sort~BT~ on a vector of them — or of a type
		  holding one — is a compile error.
		* **A map does not travel and does not encode.** ~BT~encode~BT~ refuses one
		  and so does a [~BT~hive.syslink~BT~](syslink.md) mailbox: decoding there is by
		  declared shape, while a map's keys are whatever was put in it. Send its pairs
		  as a vector of a type declared for them. The one exception is a
		  [~BT~JsonValue~BT~](json.md) object's properties, whose codec is the document itself.

		The module's name ~BT~map~BT~ **coexists with the ~BT~map(v, f)~BT~ builtin**: a call
		on the name is the module and a bare call is the builtin, and the two are told
		apart by shape.
	`)
}

func files(): Str {
	return page.backticked(`
		# ~BT~hive.file~BT~

		General filesystem access. Contents move as ~BT~Str~BT~, which holds bytes rather
		than validated text, so **a binary file survives a read/write round trip**.
		Reading a whole [~BT~Table~BT~](../tables.md) out of one — ~BT~csv~BT~, ~BT~xlsx~BT~, ~BT~ods~BT~ —
		lives here too.

		| call | answers with |
		| --- | --- |
		| ~BT~read(path)~BT~ | ~BT~Result<Str, FileError>~BT~ |
		| ~BT~lines(path)~BT~ | ~BT~Result<Str[dyn], FileError>~BT~ |
		| ~BT~write(path, contents)~BT~ | ~BT~Result<Int, FileError>~BT~ — the bytes written |
		| ~BT~append(path, contents)~BT~ | ~BT~Result<Int, FileError>~BT~ |
		| ~BT~size(path)~BT~ | ~BT~Result<Int, FileError>~BT~ |
		| ~BT~copy(from, to)~BT~ | ~BT~Result<Int, FileError>~BT~ |
		| ~BT~delete(path)~BT~ | ~BT~Result<Bool, FileError>~BT~ |
		| ~BT~makeDir(path)~BT~ | ~BT~Result<Bool, FileError>~BT~ |
		| ~BT~move(from, to)~BT~ | ~BT~Result<Bool, FileError>~BT~ |
		| ~BT~list(path)~BT~ | ~BT~Result<Str[dyn], FileError>~BT~ |
		| ~BT~exists(path)~BT~ | ~BT~Bool~BT~ — **not** a ~BT~Result~BT~; a directory counts |
		| ~BT~csv(path, separator)~BT~ | ~BT~Result<Table, TableError>~BT~ |
		| ~BT~xlsx(path)~BT~ | ~BT~Result<Table[dyn], TableError>~BT~ — one per sheet |
		| ~BT~ods(path)~BT~ | ~BT~Result<Table[dyn], TableError>~BT~ — one per table |

		A ~BT~FileError~BT~ is ~BT~NotFound~BT~, ~BT~Permission~BT~, ~BT~Exists~BT~ or ~BT~Io~BT~, each carrying the
		~BT~path~BT~ and the underlying ~BT~message~BT~. A file that
		opened and then turned out not to hold what it said it did is not a filesystem
		failure, so the readers answer with a ~BT~TableError~BT~ of their own, carrying the
		~BT~path~BT~ and a ~BT~message~BT~.

		**The separator is always written.** There are no optional parameters in Hive,
		and a comma is a choice like any other rather than the one the language makes
		on your behalf. It is one character, and a longer one is an error. Which reader
		a program names is also what lets the compiler leave the others out of the
		build: one that reads a CSV carries neither ~BT~archive/zip~BT~ nor ~BT~encoding/xml~BT~.

		A CSV's rows keep the length they have — a ragged file is a ragged ~BT~Table~BT~ — blank
		lines are skipped, and a byte-order mark at the start is dropped.

		~CODE~hive
		if hive.file.read("./notes.txt") is Result.Ok(text) \{
			echo text
		}

		if hive.file.write(path, rendered) is Result.Error(why) \{
			echo "cannot write \{path}: \{why.message}"
		}
		~CODE~

		## Worth knowing

		* ~BT~lines~BT~ splits on newlines, **dropping the empty piece a trailing newline
		  leaves** and any Windows carriage returns.
		* ~BT~write~BT~ replaces a file, creating it when absent; ~BT~append~BT~ adds to the end.
		  **Neither creates missing parent directories** — call ~BT~makeDir~BT~ first.
		* ~BT~writeSecret(path, contents)~BT~ writes a [~BT~Secret<Str>~BT~](../types.md#secret), leaving the file **readable by the user running the
		  program and nobody else**: mode ~BT~0600~BT~ on Linux and macOS, and on Windows an
		  access list naming that one account, protected so the containing folder
		  hands nothing down. Who may read it is settled **before any of the secret is
		  in it**, and a file that was already there is narrowed rather than trusted.
		  A file owned by somebody else is an ~BT~Error~BT~ rather than a secret written in
		  the clear. Permissions are all it is, so it is **not encryption**: an
		  administrator, and ~BT~root~BT~, can read any file on the machine.
		* ~BT~makeDir~BT~ creates a directory **along with any missing parents**, and is not
		  an error when it is already there.
		* ~BT~delete~BT~ removes a file, or an **already-empty** directory.
		* ~BT~list~BT~ answers **sorted and without any leading path** — the names in the
		  directory, not paths to them.

		A test runs with the working directory set to the generated project rather than
		the entrypoint's folder, so **a test that needs files should write them itself**
		with ~BT~makeDir~BT~ and ~BT~write~BT~. See [testing](../testing.md).
	`)
}

func term(): Str {
	return page.backticked(`
		# ~BT~hive.term~BT~

		Line-oriented terminal I/O. Running *another* program is
		[term-exec](term-exec.md).

		| call | answers with |
		| --- | --- |
		| ~BT~print(text)~BT~ | ~BT~void~BT~ — a line to **stdout** |
		| ~BT~status(text)~BT~ | ~BT~void~BT~ — a line to **stderr** |
		| ~BT~isTerminal()~BT~ | ~BT~Bool~BT~ |
		| ~BT~read()~BT~ | ~BT~Str~BT~ |
		| ~BT~readSecret()~BT~ | ~BT~Result<Secret<Str>, SecretError>~BT~ — see [~BT~Secret~BT~](../types.md#secret) |
		| ~BT~readSecretBypass()~BT~ | ~BT~Secret<Str>~BT~ — the same, in ordinary memory; **cannot fail** |
		| ~BT~args()~BT~ | ~BT~Str[dyn]~BT~ |
		| ~BT~codec()~BT~ | ~BT~hive.codec.Codec<FlagError>~BT~ — the value that says command-line flags |
		| ~BT~exit(code)~BT~ | ~BT~void~BT~ — nothing after it runs |

		## Which stream, and why it matters

		~BT~print~BT~ is ~BT~echo~BT~ restricted to a ~BT~Str~BT~ — the same lowering. ~BT~status~BT~ writes to
		**standard error**: what a program is *doing*, as against what it was asked for.
		Redirecting the answer never takes the progress with it, which is what lets
		~BT~hive emit x.hive > main.go~BT~ be Go while the compiler still says where it has
		got to.

		~BT~isTerminal()~BT~ is whether **standard error** is a terminal rather than a file or
		a pipe — the difference between somebody sitting there waiting and output being
		collected for later. A program that reports its own progress asks this first.

		## Input

		~BT~read()~BT~ blocks for a line, stripped of its trailing newline, and **parks only
		the calling virtual thread**. At end of input it returns whatever preceded EOF
		(~BT~""~BT~ if nothing).

		~BT~readSecret()~BT~ is that same read with the terminal's echo turned off. The echo
		is put back before the call returns, and also if the program is interrupted at
		the prompt. Where there is no terminal at all, the line is read exactly as
		~BT~read()~BT~ reads it and only the hiding of it is lost.

		~BT~readSecretBypass()~BT~ is that read into ordinary memory, as ~BT~bypass~BT~ is to
		~BT~hide~BT~: never shown, but free to be swapped out, so there is no locked-memory
		limit to run into and it answers a ~BT~Secret<Str>~BT~ rather than a ~BT~Result~BT~.

		## Arguments and exit

		~BT~args()~BT~ is the command-line arguments in order, **excluding the program
		name** — so ~BT~args()[0]~BT~ is the first thing the user typed after it.

		~BT~exit(code)~BT~ ends the program with a status. There is no value to answer with
		and nothing after it runs, which is what makes it a statement rather than a call.

		~CODE~hive
		proc main(): void \{
			args := hive.term.args()
			if len(args) < 1 \{
				hive.term.status("usage: greet <name>")
				hive.term.exit(1)
			}
			if args bounds 0 \{
				echo "hello \{args[0]}"
			}
		}
		~CODE~

		## Flags into a type

		~BT~codec()~BT~ reads **command-line flags** into one of your own types through
		~BT~T.decode~BT~, and [~BT~encode~BT~](../builtins.md) writes one back. It reads one line
		of text, split the way a shell would, so a quoted argument stays together:

		~CODE~hive
		type Options \{
			name: Str
			dryRun: Bool -- Flag as dry-run; Default as false;
			count: Int -- Default as 10;
		}

		options := Options.decode(join(hive.term.args(), " "), hive.term.codec())
		if options is Result.Error(why) \{
			hive.term.status("\{why.path}: expected \{why.expected}, found \{why.found}")
			hive.term.exit(2)
		}

		Options.decode("--name Ada --dry-run --count=3", hive.term.codec())
		encode(Options("Ada Lovelace", true, 3), hive.term.codec())
		// --name "Ada Lovelace" --dry-run true --count 3
		~CODE~

		* **A flag always opens with ~BT~--~BT~**: ~BT~--name value~BT~ or ~BT~--name=value~BT~. Its name
		  is the field's own, or what ~BT~Flag as~BT~ says without the dashes — so
		  ~BT~--something-else~BT~ is ~BT~somethingElse: Str -- Flag as something-else;~BT~.
		* A ~BT~Bool~BT~ standing alone is ~BT~true~BT~; followed by ~BT~true~BT~ or ~BT~false~BT~, it is that.
		* A vector is its flag repeated, once per element.
		* **A missing flag is an error** unless the field has a ~BT~Default~BT~ — a ~BT~Bool~BT~
		  included — and so is one given twice for a field that is not a vector.
		* Words that are no flag's value are ignored, and so is everything after a lone
		  ~BT~--~BT~. Quotes and a backslash keep a word together, and a quoted word is never
		  a flag.
		* Only a type without variants travels as flags, each field a ~BT~Str~BT~, ~BT~Int~BT~,
		  ~BT~Float~BT~, ~BT~Bool~BT~ or ~BT~Atom~BT~, or a vector of one. A failure is a
		  ~BT~hive.term.FlagError~BT~ whose ~BT~path~BT~ is the flag as written, ~BT~--count~BT~.
	`)
}

func termExec(): Str {
	return page.backticked(`
		# ~BT~hive.term~BT~ — running another program

		Three calls. The difference between the first two is **who is talking to the
		terminal**; the third is the first with an environment.

		| call | answers with |
		| --- | --- |
		| ~BT~exec(command, arguments, directory)~BT~ | ~BT~Result<Str, ExecError>~BT~ |
		| ~BT~attach(command, arguments, directory)~BT~ | ~BT~Int~BT~ — the exit status |
		| ~BT~execWith(command, arguments, directory, environment)~BT~ | ~BT~Result<Str, ExecError>~BT~ |

		~BT~arguments~BT~ is a ~BT~Str[]~BT~ and ~BT~directory~BT~ is a ~BT~Str~BT~ — empty meaning where this
		program is. ~BT~environment~BT~ is a ~BT~hive.map.Map<Str, Str>~BT~.

		**The arguments are a vector rather than a line of text, and that is the whole
		of the safety story**: nothing is handed to a shell, so nothing in an argument
		can be read as one. A command that needs a shell asks for one by name.

		## ~BT~exec~BT~ captures, ~BT~attach~BT~ hands the terminal over

		~BT~exec~BT~ hands back **everything the command wrote** — standard output and
		standard error together, in the order it wrote them, which is the order somebody
		reading a terminal would have seen. **A status other than zero is an ~BT~Error~BT~**,
		because a command that failed is not a command that answered.

		An ~BT~ExecError~BT~ carries the ~BT~command~BT~, the ~BT~code~BT~, the ~BT~output~BT~ and a ~BT~message~BT~.
		The output is on it because that is usually the whole of why it failed, and a
		command that could not be started at all has a ~BT~code~BT~ of ~BT~-1~BT~.

		~CODE~hive
		if hive.term.exec("go", ["build", "."], buildDirectory) is Result.Error(why) \{
			echo "the build failed:\n\{why.output}"
			hive.term.exit(1)
		}
		~CODE~

		~BT~attach~BT~ gives the command **this program's own terminal** — its input, its
		output, its errors — and answers with the ~BT~Int~BT~ status it exited with. Nothing
		is captured, because the point of it is that the command is talking to whoever
		started this program.

		## ~BT~execWith~BT~ is an overlay, never a replacement

		Its map's pairs are set for the command **on top of** the environment this
		program was started with. That is deliberate: a replaced environment is a much
		sharper tool — a command with no ~BT~PATH~BT~ finds no programs and one with no
		~BT~HOME~BT~ finds no caches — and nothing that wants to set one variable wants those
		as well.

		So an **empty map is exactly ~BT~exec~BT~**, byte for byte, and a name the environment
		already holds is replaced rather than joined by a second.

		~CODE~hive
		mut hive.map.Map<Str, Str> asked = hive.map.new()
		hive.map.set(asked, "GOOS", "linux")
		hive.map.set(asked, "GOARCH", "arm64")

		// ~BT~go env GOOS~BT~ now says ~BT~linux~BT~, and ~BT~go~BT~ is still found on the PATH.
		hive.term.execWith("go", ["build", "."], buildDirectory, asked)
		~CODE~

		This is what ~BT~hive build --target~BT~ is built on.
	`)
}

func env(): Str {
	return page.backticked(`
		# ~BT~hive.env~BT~

		~CODE~hive
		if hive.env.get("DATABASE_URL") is Result.Ok(url) \{
			echo url
		}
		~CODE~

		~BT~get(name)~BT~ → ~BT~Result<Str, hive.env.EnvironmentError>~BT~, whose error carries
		the ~BT~key~BT~ and a ~BT~message~BT~. ~BT~getSecret(name)~BT~ is the same lookup answering a
		[~BT~Secret<Str>~BT~](../types.md#secret): the program's copy is locked and unprintable, though
		the environment and the ~BT~.env~BT~ file still hold it as text.

		**It resolves in this order:**

		1. the ~BT~.env~BT~ file beside the executable;
		2. the one in the working directory — the entrypoint's folder, under ~BT~hive run~BT~;
		3. the ~BT~.env~BT~ in the parent folder of either;
		4. the OS environment.

		So a value put in a nearby ~BT~.env~BT~ wins over one exported in the shell.

		The ~BT~.env~BT~ file is read **once**, on the first ~BT~get~BT~. Blank lines and ~BT~#~BT~
		comments are ignored, an optional ~BT~export ~BT~ prefix is allowed, and a value may
		be wrapped in quotes, which are stripped.

		~CODE~
		# a comment
		export DATABASE_URL="postgres://localhost/hive"
		PORT=8080
		~CODE~

		A container hands variables over with ~BT~docker run -e~BT~ and ~BT~get~BT~ reads those the
		same way, which is why a ~BT~.env~BT~ holding anything private belongs in a
		~BT~.dockerignore~BT~.
	`)
}

func task(): Str {
	return page.backticked(`
		# ~BT~hive.task~BT~

		The module is three things, and one of them has no call.

		## ~BT~sleep(ms)~BT~

		Parks the calling virtual thread. **Only that goroutine waits**, so two calls
		that each sleep, waited for together in one ~BT~await~BT~, finish in about the longer
		of the two rather than the sum:

		~CODE~hive
		await [slowly("a"), slowly("b")]   // one second, not two
		~CODE~

		A non-positive ~BT~ms~BT~ returns immediately.

		## ~BT~sleepForever()~BT~

		Parks the calling virtual thread for good — for a ~BT~main~BT~ that has started what
		the program is for and has nothing left to do itself. The program still ends
		where it would have: at ~BT~hive.term.exit~BT~, or when its last window closes.

		## ~BT~TimeoutError~BT~

		The type ~BT~with timeout~BT~ answers with. It carries ~BT~waited~BT~ — the milliseconds
		that were asked for, an ~BT~Int~BT~ — and a ~BT~message~BT~.

		~CODE~hive
		if slowShout("worth waiting for") with timeout 100 is Result.Error(err) \{
			echo "gave up after \{err.waited}ms"
		}
		~CODE~

		~BT~with timeout~BT~ is a language clause rather than a call, and lives on
		[concurrency](../concurrency.md). In short: it may follow anything that waits,
		turns the result into ~BT~Result<T, hive.task.TimeoutError>~BT~, is **one deadline
		across a whole await-all**, and abandons the **waiting rather than the work** —
		the call runs on and only its result is dropped.
	`)
}

func times(): Str {
	return page.backticked(`
		# ~BT~hive.time~BT~

		**Times are plain ~BT~Int~BT~s — Unix milliseconds.** There is no date type to build, no
		duration type to convert, and arithmetic on a time is arithmetic.

		| call | answers with |
		| --- | --- |
		| ~BT~now()~BT~ | ~BT~Int~BT~ |
		| ~BT~timezone()~BT~ | ~BT~Str~BT~ — the zone name or abbreviation at this instant |
		| ~BT~timezoneOffset()~BT~ | ~BT~Int~BT~ — minutes east of UTC |
		| ~BT~format(time, template)~BT~ | ~BT~Str~BT~ — rendered in **local** time |
		| ~BT~dateFrom(year, month, day)~BT~ | ~BT~Int~BT~ — that date at midnight |
		| ~BT~dateTimeFrom(year, month, day, hour, minute, second)~BT~ | ~BT~Int~BT~ — that instant |

		~BT~format~BT~ takes a ~BT~strftime~BT~-style template. Unrecognised ~BT~%x~BT~ escapes pass
		through verbatim:

		| | | | |
		|---|---|---|---|
		| ~BT~%Y~BT~ year (4) | ~BT~%y~BT~ year (2) | ~BT~%m~BT~ month | ~BT~%d~BT~ day |
		| ~BT~%H~BT~ hour 00–23 | ~BT~%I~BT~ hour 01–12 | ~BT~%M~BT~ minute | ~BT~%S~BT~ second |
		| ~BT~%p~BT~ AM/PM | ~BT~%j~BT~ day-of-year | ~BT~%Z~BT~ zone name | ~BT~%z~BT~ zone offset |
		| ~BT~%A~BT~/~BT~%a~BT~ weekday | ~BT~%B~BT~/~BT~%b~BT~ month name | ~BT~%%~BT~ literal ~BT~%~BT~ | |

		~CODE~hive
		echo hive.time.format(hive.time.now(), "%Y-%m-%d %H:%M")
		echo hive.time.format(stamp, "%A %e %B")           // %e passes through
		elapsed := hive.time.now() - started               // milliseconds, as an Int
		~CODE~

		## Building a time

		~BT~dateFrom~BT~ and ~BT~dateTimeFrom~BT~ answer with the same kind of ~BT~Int~BT~ ~BT~now()~BT~ does,
		for a date you name rather than this one. Both build in **local** time, so a
		stamp round-trips with ~BT~format~BT~.

		A field out of range **carries into the next one**, the way a clock does:
		month 13 is January of the year after, and day 0 is the last day of the month
		before. That is not a mistake to work around — it is how a boundary is reached
		without a calendar:

		~CODE~hive
		hive.time.dateFrom(2026, 9, 14)                  // 2026-09-14 00:00:00
		hive.time.dateTimeFrom(2026, 9, 14, 13, 30, 0)   // 2026-09-14 13:30:00
		hive.time.dateFrom(2026, 13, 1)                  // 2027-01-01
		hive.time.dateFrom(2026, 3, 0)                   // 2026-02-28, February's last
		~CODE~

		## The current instant, field by field

		Each answers one field of **now**, read in local time. They take no arguments:
		to read a field off some other stamp, render that stamp with ~BT~format~BT~.

		| call | answers |
		| --- | --- |
		| ~BT~year()~BT~ | the full year — ~BT~2026~BT~ |
		| ~BT~month()~BT~ | ~BT~1~BT~–~BT~12~BT~ |
		| ~BT~day()~BT~ | ~BT~1~BT~–~BT~31~BT~ |
		| ~BT~weekday()~BT~ | ~BT~1~BT~ Monday – ~BT~7~BT~ Sunday, the ISO numbering |
		| ~BT~lastDayOfMonth()~BT~ | ~BT~28~BT~–~BT~31~BT~ — how long **this** month is |
		| ~BT~hour()~BT~ | ~BT~0~BT~–~BT~23~BT~ |
		| ~BT~minute()~BT~, ~BT~second()~BT~ | ~BT~0~BT~–~BT~59~BT~ |
		| ~BT~millisecond()~BT~ | ~BT~0~BT~–~BT~999~BT~ |

		Together they are the ends of the current month, which needs no calendar of
		your own:

		~CODE~hive
		year := hive.time.year()
		month := hive.time.month()
		first := hive.time.dateFrom(year, month, 1)
		last := hive.time.dateFrom(year, month, hive.time.lastDayOfMonth())
		~CODE~

		A stamp is in milliseconds, the unit ~BT~with timeout~BT~ and
		[~BT~hive.task.sleep~BT~](task.md) speak too. ~BT~millisecond()~BT~ is something else: the
		sub-second **field** of this instant, the way ~BT~second()~BT~ reads ~BT~0~BT~–~BT~59~BT~.
	`)
}

func json(): Str {
	return page.backticked(`
		# ~BT~hive.json~BT~

		Built on the idea that **Hive's type declarations *are* the JSON schema**. There
		is no schema to write, no tag to annotate a field with, and no reflection: a
		decoder for a type is derived at compile time.

		| call | answers with |
		| --- | --- |
		| ~BT~hive.json.codec()~BT~ | ~BT~hive.codec.Codec<JsonError>~BT~ — the value that says JSON |
		| ~BT~T.decode(text, codec)~BT~ | ~BT~Result<T, E>~BT~ — ~BT~E~BT~ is the codec's error, ~BT~JsonError~BT~ here |
		| ~BT~T.Variant.decode(text, codec)~BT~ | the same — one variant's payload |
		| ~BT~encode(value, codec)~BT~ | ~BT~Str~BT~ — **cannot fail** |
		| ~BT~JsonValue.decode(text, codec)~BT~ | ~BT~Result<JsonValue, _>~BT~ — any document |

		A ~BT~JsonError~BT~ carries the exact ~BT~path~BT~ that failed, what was ~BT~expected~BT~
		and what was ~BT~found~BT~. ~BT~encode~BT~ and ~BT~decode~BT~ name no format: **the codec is
		the only thing that says JSON**, which is what lets another format be another
		codec — [~BT~jwtCodec~BT~](crypto-jwt.md), [~BT~queryParamsCodec~BT~](net-urls.md) and
		[~BT~hive.term.codec~BT~](term.md) are —
		rather than a change to the language. A codec's type names the error its
		decoding fails with, ~BT~hive.codec.Codec<E>~BT~, and each module owns its own.

		A codec is an **ordinary value**: its type already says the format, so one held
		in a variable, passed to a function or kept in a field encodes and decodes
		exactly as one written at the call.

		~CODE~hive
		type User \{
			id:   Int
			name: Str
		}

		if User.decode(body, hive.json.codec()) is Result.Ok(user) \{
			echo user.name
		}
		echo encode(user, hive.json.codec())
		~CODE~

		## The annotations steer both codecs

		A field's ~BT~--~BT~ annotations are what decide the key and what happens when it is
		not there — see [declarations](../declarations.md#field-annotations):

		~CODE~hive
		type User \{
			name: Str -- JSON as username;
			age:  Int
			firstName: Str -- Default as "";
		}

		encode(User("ada", 36, "Ada"), hive.json.codec())
		// \{"username":"ada","age":36,"firstName":"Ada"}

		User.decode("\\{\"username\":\"ada\",\"age\":36}", hive.json.codec())
		// Result.Ok — ~BT~firstName~BT~ fell back to ""
		~CODE~

		~BT~JSON as~BT~ renames in **both** directions, so a round trip agrees with itself.
		~BT~Default as~BT~ is what turns a required field into an optional one: an absent
		key and a ~BT~null~BT~ one are the same nothing, and both read as the literal.

		## Decoding

		* **Missing fields, wrong types and wrong static vector lengths become errors**
		  carrying the path that failed — unless the field has a ~BT~Default~BT~, which
		  covers the first of those and **not** the second.
		* **JSON fields the type doesn't declare are ignored.**
		* **Variants decode as ~BT~\{"VariantName": \{...}}~BT~**, and JSON ~BT~null~BT~ selects a
		  type's first field-less variant.
		* ~BT~encode~BT~ derives the encoder from the **static** type and therefore cannot
		  fail — it answers with a ~BT~Str~BT~, not a ~BT~Result~BT~.
		* **A map does not encode.** Its keys are whatever was put in it, while decoding
		  here is by declared shape. Send its pairs as a vector of a type declared for them
		  — or hold the document as a ~BT~JsonValue~BT~, below, whose objects are maps.
		* **Nor does a ~BT~Table~BT~**, anywhere in a value: it is rows of cells with nothing
		  to name them. ~BT~Table.decode~BT~ does not exist.

		## When the shape is not yours to declare

		~BT~hive.json.JsonValue~BT~ is any document, as the JSON it is:

		~CODE~hive
		type JsonValue \{
			String \{ value: Str }
			Boolean \{ value: Bool }
			Int \{ value: Int }
			Float \{ value: Float }
			Array \{ values: JsonValue[dyn] }
			Object \{ properties: hive.map.Map<Str, JsonValue> }
			Null
		}
		~CODE~

		It reads and writes the document itself — ~BT~"x"~BT~, never
		~BT~\{"String": \{"value": "x"}}~BT~ — and is taken apart like any union:

		~CODE~hive
		if hive.json.JsonValue.decode(config, hive.json.codec()) is Result.Ok(doc) \{
			if doc is hive.json.JsonValue.Object(properties) \{
				for each key in hive.map.keys(properties) \{
					echo key
				}
				if hive.map.get(properties, "name") is Result.Ok(name) \{
					echo name
				}
			}
		}
		~CODE~

		* A number is an ~BT~Int~BT~ where it reads as one and a ~BT~Float~BT~ otherwise, so
		  ~BT~1.0~BT~ stays a ~BT~Float~BT~ both ways.
		* An object's properties are a [map](map.md), in the order the document wrote
		  them. A key written twice keeps its first place and its last value — as it
		  does for a declared type — and text after the document is an error.
		* Holding a map, a ~BT~JsonValue~BT~ has no order: ~BT~sort~BT~ over them takes the
		  ~BT~func~BT~ that says which comes first.
		* ~BT~null~BT~ is ~BT~Null()~BT~, wherever the document holds one.
		* It is read whole: the document says which variant it is, so
		  ~BT~JsonValue.String.decode~BT~ is refused.
		* A field may be one, which leaves that part of a declared type undeclared.

		The same derived codecs are what a [~BT~hive.syslink~BT~](syslink.md) message
		crosses the wire as.
	`)
}

// --- hive.crypto -------------------------------------------------------------

func crypto(): Str {
	return page.backticked(`
		# ~BT~hive.crypto~BT~ — hashing, encoding, random

		Pure, so all of it works in a ~BT~func~BT~ too. Fallible operations return
		~BT~Result<_, hive.crypto.CryptoError>~BT~: ~BT~Malformed~BT~, ~BT~BadSignature~BT~, or
		~BT~LimitExceeded~BT~ or ~BT~Unsupported~BT~ where a ~BT~Secret~BT~ could not be locked, each with a
		~BT~message~BT~.

		Encryption is [crypto-encrypt](crypto-encrypt.md); tokens are
		[crypto-jwt](crypto-jwt.md).

		| call | answers with |
		| --- | --- |
		| ~BT~sha256(input)~BT~ | ~BT~Str~BT~ — lowercase hex |
		| ~BT~sha512(input)~BT~ | ~BT~Str~BT~ — lowercase hex |
		| ~BT~hmacSha256(input, key: Secret<Str>)~BT~ | ~BT~Str~BT~ — lowercase hex |
		| ~BT~base64Encode(input)~BT~ | ~BT~Str~BT~ |
		| ~BT~base64Decode(input)~BT~ | ~BT~Result<Str, CryptoError>~BT~ |
		| ~BT~randomHex(bytes)~BT~ | ~BT~Str~BT~ — ~BT~bytes~BT~ random bytes, as hex |
		| ~BT~randomSecret(bytes)~BT~ | ~BT~Result<Secret<Str>, SecretError>~BT~ — the same, never a ~BT~Str~BT~ |

		~CODE~hive
		echo hive.crypto.sha256("the message")
		echo hive.crypto.hmacSha256(payload, bypass("a literal key"))
		token := hive.crypto.randomHex(16)          // 32 hex characters

		if hive.crypto.base64Decode(field) is Result.Ok(raw) \{
			echo len(raw)
		}
		~CODE~

		A key is a ~BT~Secret<Str>~BT~; everything else is a ~BT~Str~BT~, and a ~BT~Str~BT~ holds bytes rather
		than validated text — so hashing a file read with
		[~BT~hive.file.read~BT~](file.md) hashes the file, not a rendering of it.

		**Only ~BT~base64Decode~BT~ can fail here.** A hash of anything is a hash, and
		~BT~randomHex~BT~ draws from the platform's own source.
	`)
}

func cryptoEncrypt(): Str {
	return page.backticked(`
		# ~BT~hive.crypto~BT~ — encryption

		| call | answers with |
		| --- | --- |
		| ~BT~encrypt(plaintext: Secret<Str>, password: Secret<Str>)~BT~ | ~BT~Str~BT~ — base64; **cannot fail** |
		| ~BT~decrypt(ciphertext, password: Secret<Str>)~BT~ | ~BT~Result<Secret<Str>, CryptoError>~BT~ |
		| ~BT~decryptBypass(ciphertext, password: Secret<Str>)~BT~ | ~BT~Result<Secret<Str>, CryptoError>~BT~ — the same, in ordinary memory |

		The plaintext and the password are [~BT~Secret<Str>~BT~s](../types.md#secret), and so is what
		~BT~decrypt~BT~ opens: it goes from locked memory into AES-GCM and back without
		ever being a ~BT~Str~BT~. The ciphertext is a ~BT~Str~BT~, safe to print and store.

		~CODE~hive
		if hive.term.readSecret() is Result.Ok(password) \{
			sealed := hive.crypto.encrypt(bypass("attack at dawn"), password)

			if hive.crypto.decrypt(sealed, password) is Result.Ok(opened) \{
				echo reveal(opened)
			} else if hive.crypto.decrypt(sealed, password) is Result.Error(crypto.CryptoError.BadSignature(_)) \{
				echo "wrong password, or the message was changed"
			}
		}
		~CODE~

		~BT~decrypt~BT~ fails as ~BT~LimitExceeded~BT~ or ~BT~Unsupported~BT~ where there is no locked
		memory to open the plaintext into. ~BT~decryptBypass~BT~ opens it into ordinary memory
		instead, as ~BT~bypass~BT~ is to ~BT~hide~BT~: free to be swapped out, so it fails only as
		~BT~Malformed~BT~ or ~BT~BadSignature~BT~.

		## What it does, exactly

		~BT~encrypt~BT~ seals under a **password** with **AES-256-GCM**, base64-encoded. The
		key is derived with **600,000 rounds of PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA256** over a random salt,
		and **the salt and nonce are drawn afresh every call** — so the same text under
		the same password never encrypts alike. That is a property to expect rather than
		a bug to chase: two ciphertexts of one message are supposed to differ.

		GCM's tag travels with the ciphertext, so **an edited message is rejected rather
		than opened into something else**. That is reported as ~BT~"BadSignature"~BT~, which
		is also what a **wrong password** gives — the two are indistinguishable by
		design, and a program should not try to tell a user which it was.

		The rounds are what make this safe to use with something a human typed, and they
		are also what make it slow on purpose: a call is not free, so derive once and
		hold what you got rather than encrypting field by field in a loop.

		For **integrity without secrecy** — proving a message was not altered by someone
		who has the key — reach for ~BT~hmacSha256~BT~ on [crypto](crypto.md) instead. For a
		**claim somebody else has to read**, [crypto-jwt](crypto-jwt.md).
	`)
}

func cryptoJwt(): Str {
	return page.backticked(`
		# ~BT~hive.crypto~BT~ — JWT

		| call | answers with |
		| --- | --- |
		| ~BT~jwtCodec(secret: Secret<Str>)~BT~ | ~BT~hive.codec.Codec<JwtError>~BT~ — HS256 under this secret |
		| ~BT~jwtDecode(token)~BT~ | ~BT~Result<Str, CryptoError>~BT~ — **without verifying** |
		| ~BT~jwtHeader(token)~BT~ | ~BT~Result<hive.crypto.Header, CryptoError>~BT~ |

		A token is **a format like any other**, so there is nothing here to sign or
		verify with: ~BT~jwtCodec~BT~ is a codec beside [~BT~hive.json.codec~BT~](json.md), and
		[~BT~encode~BT~](../builtins.md) and ~BT~T.decode~BT~ are what a program
		writes and reads one with. The claims are **one of your own types**, so a
		token's payload is a type declaration rather than a bag of strings.

		~CODE~hive
		type Claims \{
			sub: Str
			exp: Int
		}

		token := encode(Claims("ada", hive.time.now() / 1000 + 3600), hive.crypto.jwtCodec(secret))

		if Claims.decode(token, hive.crypto.jwtCodec(secret)) is Result.Ok(claims) \{
			echo claims.sub
		}
		~CODE~

		## What the codec checks

		Decoding checks the signature **and the ~BT~exp~BT~/~BT~nbf~BT~ claims** before it reads
		anything. **Only HS256 is accepted**, so ~BT~alg: none~BT~ and algorithm confusion
		are rejected outright rather than negotiated.

		A token that does not check out is a ~BT~hive.crypto.JwtError~BT~, like claims of the
		wrong shape, and its ~BT~found~BT~ opens with the reason — ~BT~"Expired"~BT~,
		~BT~"BadSignature"~BT~, ~BT~"AlgorithmMismatch"~BT~ or ~BT~"Malformed"~BT~. A forged token and
		claims of the wrong shape are then one failure to handle rather than two,
		which is what a caller was going to do with them anyway.

		~BT~jwtDecode~BT~ decodes **without verifying anything at all**, and is for
		inspection only — logging what a token claims, reading a ~BT~sub~BT~ before deciding
		which secret to check it with. **Never authorise on what it answers.**

		~BT~jwtHeader~BT~ reads ~BT~alg~BT~, ~BT~typ~BT~ and ~BT~kid~BT~ off the token, which is what a
		program rotating keys needs before it can pick the secret.

		~BT~exp~BT~ and ~BT~nbf~BT~ are Unix seconds, as RFC 7519 has them, and
		[~BT~hive.time.now()~BT~](time.md) answers milliseconds — so ~BT~hive.time.now() / 1000~BT~.
	`)
}

// --- hive.net ----------------------------------------------------------------

func net(): Str {
	return page.backticked(`
		# ~BT~hive.net~BT~

		HTTP, WebSockets and raw TCP, clients and servers. Everything here performs I/O,
		so it works inside a ~BT~func~BT~ or a ~BT~proc~BT~, and **none of it adds a dependency**.

		| page | what is on it |
		| --- | --- |
		| [net-http](net-http.md) | ~BT~httpRequest~BT~, ~BT~httpServe~BT~, and the two types |
		| [net-websockets](net-websockets.md) | ~BT~wsConnect~BT~, ~BT~wsServe~BT~, ~BT~wsSend~BT~, ~BT~wsReceive~BT~ |
		| [net-sockets](net-sockets.md) | ~BT~socketConnect~BT~, ~BT~socketServe~BT~, and the stream |
		| [net-names](net-names.md) | ~BT~resolve~BT~, ~BT~localAddress~BT~ |
		| [net-urls](net-urls.md) | ~BT~urlEncode~BT~, ~BT~queryParamsCodec~BT~ |

		## What every server here has in common

		**Each of the three servers blocks forever**, so it usually goes on a virtual
		thread of its own:

		~CODE~hive
		async hive.net.httpServe(8080, handle)
		~CODE~

		Each runs **its handler once per request or connection, on a thread of its
		own** — so a slow handler holds up nobody but its own caller, and a handler
		needs no lock only because it shares nothing. Long-lived shared state belongs
		in a [service](syslink.md).

		**A handler is passed by name**, and its declared shape is checked at compile
		time — including through a partial application, which is how a handler reaches
		anything it needs:

		~CODE~hive
		proc handle(db: hive.sql.SqlConnection, req: hive.net.HttpRequest): hive.net.HttpResponse \{
			// ...
		}

		async hive.net.httpServe(8080, handle(db, _))
		~CODE~

		**Every call names its protocol**, so nothing reads as "the" default one: there
		is no ~BT~serve~BT~, only ~BT~httpServe~BT~, ~BT~wsServe~BT~ and ~BT~socketServe~BT~.

		## The errors

		Each protocol has its own error type, and each is a union with a variant per way
		it fails, every one carrying a ~BT~message~BT~:

		| type | its variants |
		| --- | --- |
		| ~BT~HttpError~BT~ | none — a struct carrying the ~BT~url~BT~ and a ~BT~message~BT~ |
		| ~BT~WsError~BT~ | ~BT~Handshake~BT~ ~BT~Protocol~BT~ ~BT~Closed~BT~ ~BT~Send~BT~ ~BT~Receive~BT~ |
		| ~BT~SocketError~BT~ | ~BT~Connect~BT~ ~BT~Closed~BT~ ~BT~Send~BT~ ~BT~Receive~BT~ |
		| ~BT~NetError~BT~ | ~BT~NotFound~BT~ ~BT~Lookup~BT~ ~BT~NoAddress~BT~ |

		For a server that answers with a **page rather than a string**, ~BT~hive.ui.page~BT~
		renders the same view tree a window paints: see [ui](ui.md).
	`)
}

func netHttp(): Str {
	return page.backticked(`
		# ~BT~hive.net~BT~ — HTTP

		| call | answers with |
		| --- | --- |
		| ~BT~httpRequest(req)~BT~ | ~BT~Result<HttpResponse, HttpError>~BT~ |
		| ~BT~httpServe(port, handler)~BT~ | ~BT~void~BT~ — **blocks forever** |

		~BT~handler~BT~ is a ~BT~proc(HttpRequest): HttpResponse~BT~.

		## The two types

		~CODE~hive
		hive.net.HttpRequest(method, url, headers, body)     // Str, Str, Table, Str
		hive.net.HttpResponse(status, headers, body)         // Int, Table, Str
		~CODE~

		**Headers are a ~BT~Table~BT~ of ~BT~[name, value]~BT~ rows**, which is what makes
		~BT~row(req.headers, "content-type")~BT~ the way to read one.

		An ~BT~HttpError~BT~ carries the ~BT~url~BT~ and a ~BT~message~BT~.

		## A client

		~CODE~hive
		asked := hive.net.HttpRequest("GET", "https://example.com/api", [], "")

		if hive.net.httpRequest(asked) is Result.Ok(answer) \{
			if answer.status == 200 \{
				echo answer.body
			}
		}
		~CODE~

		A ~BT~Result.Ok~BT~ means **the round trip happened**, not that the server was
		pleased: a 404 and a 500 are both an ~BT~Ok~BT~ carrying that status. An ~BT~Error~BT~ is a
		request that never got an answer at all.

		Several requests at once are the [await-all](../concurrency.md):

		~CODE~hive
		both := await [hive.net.httpRequest(a), hive.net.httpRequest(b)]
		~CODE~

		## A server

		~CODE~hive
		proc handle(req: hive.net.HttpRequest): hive.net.HttpResponse \{
			if req.url is "/health" \{
				return hive.net.HttpResponse(200, [], "ok")
			}
			if req.url is "/users/\{id}" \{
				return hive.net.HttpResponse(200, [], "user \{id}")
			}
			return hive.net.HttpResponse(404, [], "not found")
		}

		proc main(): void \{
			hive.net.httpServe(8080, handle)
		}
		~CODE~

		Routing is [string patterns](../patterns.md) on ~BT~req.url~BT~ — that is what they
		are for, and a hole between two ~BT~/~BT~ never swallows a ~BT~/~BT~.

		~BT~httpServe~BT~ blocks forever, so a program doing anything else puts it on a
		thread: ~BT~async hive.net.httpServe(8080, handle)~BT~. It runs ~BT~handle~BT~ once per
		request, on a thread of its own.
	`)
}

func netWebsockets(): Str {
	return page.backticked(`
		# ~BT~hive.net~BT~ — WebSockets

		RFC 6455. **The handshake, frame headers, masking, ping/pong and fragmentation
		are the runtime's business; a program sees whole messages as ~BT~Str~BT~.**

		| call | answers with |
		| --- | --- |
		| ~BT~wsConnect(url)~BT~ | ~BT~Result<WsConnection, WsError>~BT~ |
		| ~BT~wsServe(port, handler)~BT~ | ~BT~void~BT~ — **blocks forever** |
		| ~BT~wsSend(conn, message)~BT~ | ~BT~Result<Int, WsError>~BT~ — the bytes it carried |
		| ~BT~wsReceive(conn)~BT~ | ~BT~Result<Str, WsError>~BT~ — the next message |
		| ~BT~wsRequest(conn)~BT~ | ~BT~HttpRequest~BT~ — the request that opened it |
		| ~BT~wsClose(conn)~BT~ | ~BT~void~BT~ |

		~BT~handler~BT~ is a ~BT~proc(WsConnection): void~BT~, run once per connection on a thread
		of its own. A ~BT~WsConnection~BT~ is **opaque** — a handle to hold and hand back.

		A ~BT~WsError~BT~ is ~BT~Handshake~BT~, ~BT~Protocol~BT~, ~BT~Closed~BT~, ~BT~Send~BT~ or ~BT~Receive~BT~.
		**A peer that hangs up is ~BT~WsError.Closed~BT~ — the ordinary end of a
		conversation**, not a failure to report.

		## A server

		~CODE~hive
		proc echoBack(conn: hive.net.WsConnection): void \{
			for ; true; \{
				if hive.net.wsReceive(conn) is Result.Ok(message) \{
					if hive.net.wsSend(conn, message) is Result.Error(_) \{
						return
					}
				} else \{
					return                       // "Closed": they hung up
				}
			}
		}

		proc main(): void \{
			hive.net.wsServe(9001, echoBack)
		}
		~CODE~

		~BT~wsRequest(conn)~BT~ is how a server reads **the path and the headers it was
		reached at** — the URL a browser opened, and any token in it.

		## A client

		~CODE~hive
		if hive.net.wsConnect("wss://example.com/feed") is Result.Ok(conn) \{
			_ := hive.net.wsSend(conn, "subscribe")
			if hive.net.wsReceive(conn) is Result.Ok(first) \{
				echo first
			}
			hive.net.wsClose(conn)
		}
		~CODE~

		A receive **blocks the calling virtual thread** and nobody else, so a client
		that also does something else reads on a thread of its own.
	`)
}

func netSockets(): Str {
	return page.backticked(`
		# ~BT~hive.net~BT~ — raw TCP

		**A stream, not a queue of messages.** Nothing here preserves the boundaries a
		program wrote in: two sends may arrive as one read, and one send as two.

		| call | answers with |
		| --- | --- |
		| ~BT~socketConnect(host, port)~BT~ | ~BT~Result<SocketConnection, SocketError>~BT~ |
		| ~BT~socketServe(port, handler)~BT~ | ~BT~void~BT~ — **blocks forever** |
		| ~BT~socketSend(conn, data)~BT~ | ~BT~Result<Int, SocketError>~BT~ |
		| ~BT~socketReceive(conn, bytes)~BT~ | ~BT~Result<Str, SocketError>~BT~ |
		| ~BT~socketReceiveLine(conn)~BT~ | ~BT~Result<Str, SocketError>~BT~ — without the newline |
		| ~BT~socketPeer(conn)~BT~ | ~BT~Str~BT~ |
		| ~BT~socketClose(conn)~BT~ | ~BT~void~BT~ |

		~BT~handler~BT~ is a ~BT~proc(SocketConnection): void~BT~, run once per connection on a
		thread of its own. A ~BT~SocketConnection~BT~ is **opaque**.

		A ~BT~SocketError~BT~ is ~BT~Connect~BT~, ~BT~Closed~BT~, ~BT~Send~BT~ or ~BT~Receive~BT~.

		## A short read is normal

		~BT~socketReceive(conn, 4096)~BT~ asks for **at most** that many bytes and answers
		with whatever had arrived. That is the stream being a stream rather than
		something going wrong, so a program that needs a whole thing loops until it has
		one.

		**~BT~socketReceiveLine~BT~ is the usual answer to that**, because a line is a
		boundary the other end actually wrote:

		~CODE~hive
		proc serve(conn: hive.net.SocketConnection): void \{
			echo "connection from \{hive.net.socketPeer(conn)}"
			for ; true; \{
				if hive.net.socketReceiveLine(conn) is Result.Ok(line) \{
					_ := hive.net.socketSend(conn, "you said: \{line}\n")
				} else \{
					return
				}
			}
		}

		proc main(): void \{
			hive.net.socketServe(7000, serve)
		}
		~CODE~

		~BT~socketPeer~BT~ is the far end's address, which is what a log line wants.

		For a protocol of whole messages, prefer
		[WebSockets](net-websockets.md); for one between Hive programs, a
		[service](syslink.md) is already framed, typed and encrypted.
	`)
}

func netNames(): Str {
	return page.backticked(`
		# ~BT~hive.net~BT~ — names and addresses

		| call | answers with |
		| --- | --- |
		| ~BT~resolve(name)~BT~ | ~BT~Result<Str[dyn], NetError>~BT~ |
		| ~BT~localAddress()~BT~ | ~BT~Result<Str, NetError>~BT~ |

		A ~BT~NetError~BT~ is ~BT~NotFound~BT~, ~BT~Lookup~BT~ or ~BT~NoAddress~BT~.

		## ~BT~resolve~BT~

		**Every** address behind a name, **in resolver order** — not the first one, and
		not one picked for you. It takes a **name, not an endpoint**, so no port goes in
		it, and **an address literal resolves to itself**.

		~CODE~hive
		if hive.net.resolve("example.com") is Result.Ok(addresses) \{
			for each address in addresses \{
				echo address
			}
		}
		~CODE~

		Handing back all of them is what lets a program try the next one when the first
		refuses, and what lets one that cares about families choose.

		## ~BT~localAddress~BT~

		The address **other machines reach this one on**: the source address the OS
		would stamp on a packet leaving by the default route.

		~CODE~hive
		if hive.net.localAddress() is Result.Ok(here) \{
			echo "listening on \{here}:9000"
		}
		~CODE~

		**Nothing is sent to find out**, and **loopback is deliberately not an answer** —
		~BT~127.0.0.1~BT~ is not an address anybody else can use, so a machine that has only
		that answers ~BT~NetError.NoAddress~BT~ rather than something misleading.

		This is what a [node](syslink-nodes.md) prints so another can dial it.
	`)
}

func netUrls(): Str {
	return page.backticked(`
		# ~BT~hive.net~BT~ — URLs and query strings

		| call | answers with |
		| --- | --- |
		| ~BT~urlEncode(text)~BT~ | ~BT~Str~BT~ — safe in a path segment and a query alike |
		| ~BT~queryParamsCodec()~BT~ | ~BT~hive.codec.Codec<QueryParamsError>~BT~ — the value that says query string |

		Neither performs I/O.

		## ~BT~urlEncode~BT~

		Every byte but letters, digits, ~BT~-~BT~, ~BT~.~BT~, ~BT~_~BT~ and the tilde is
		percent-encoded, so a space is ~BT~%20~BT~ rather than ~BT~+~BT~:

		~CODE~hive
		hive.net.urlEncode("rust & go")   // rust%20%26%20go
		~CODE~

		## A query string is a codec

		~BT~queryParamsCodec~BT~ goes where [~BT~hive.json.codec()~BT~](json.md) goes, so a type's
		fields are written with [~BT~encode~BT~](../builtins.md) and read with ~BT~T.decode~BT~ —
		**without** the leading ~BT~?~BT~:

		~CODE~hive
		type Search \{
			term: Str -- URL as q;
			page: Int -- Default as 1;
			tags: Str[dyn] -- URL as tag;
		}

		encode(Search("rust & go", 2, ["web", "cli"]), hive.net.queryParamsCodec())
		// q=rust%20%26%20go&page=2&tag=web&tag=cli

		if Search.decode("q=hive+lang&tag=web", hive.net.queryParamsCodec()) is Result.Ok(search) \{
			echo search.term   // hive lang, on page 1
		}
		~CODE~

		* **~BT~URL as~BT~ names the parameter**, the way ~BT~JSON as~BT~ names a key; without it
		  the field's own name is the key. Two fields may not answer to one.
		* **A vector is its key repeated**, once per element and in order. An absent key
		  reads as an empty vector, and a ~BT~T[n]~BT~ needs it exactly ~BT~n~BT~ times.
		* **A missing field is an error** unless it has a ~BT~Default~BT~, and so is a key
		  given twice for a field that is not a vector.
		* ~BT~+~BT~ and ~BT~%20~BT~ both read as a space, a leading ~BT~?~BT~ is tolerated, and a
		  parameter the type does not declare is ignored. A ~BT~Bool~BT~ is ~BT~true~BT~ or
		  ~BT~false~BT~.

		## What travels as one

		A query string is **flat**, so only a type without variants does, and each of its
		fields is a ~BT~Str~BT~, ~BT~Int~BT~, ~BT~Float~BT~, ~BT~Bool~BT~ or ~BT~Atom~BT~, or a vector of one.
		Anything else is a compile error at the ~BT~encode~BT~ or ~BT~decode~BT~ that asked.

		A failure is a ~BT~hive.net.QueryParamsError~BT~: the ~BT~path~BT~ that failed, such as
		~BT~$.page~BT~, what was ~BT~expected~BT~ there, and what was ~BT~found~BT~.
	`)
}

// --- hive.sql ----------------------------------------------------------------

func sql(): Str {
	return page.backticked(`
		# ~BT~hive.sql~BT~

		Talks to **SQLite** (the pure-Go ~BT~modernc.org/sqlite~BT~ driver, compiled straight
		into your executable) and **PostgreSQL** (~BT~github.com/lib/pq~BT~). This is the one
		module that links a dependency, fetched on the first build that needs it.

		| call | answers with |
		| --- | --- |
		| ~BT~connect(driver, connString)~BT~ | ~BT~Result<SqlConnection, SqlError>~BT~ |
		| ~BT~pool(driver, connString, maxOpen, maxIdle)~BT~ | ~BT~Result<SqlConnection, SqlError>~BT~ |
		| ~BT~close(conn)~BT~ | ~BT~void~BT~ |
		| ~BT~run(conn, fragment)~BT~ | whatever the query declared its rows to be |
		| ~BT~raw(conn, text)~BT~ | ~BT~Result<Table, SqlError>~BT~ |

		The driver is a ~BT~hive.sql.DatabaseDriver~BT~, built with ~BT~.SQLite()~BT~,
		~BT~.PostgreSQL()~BT~ or ~BT~.Other(name)~BT~. A ~BT~SqlConnection~BT~ is **opaque**.

		**Opening a connection and running what a query built is the whole of this
		module's surface.** Everything else a program does with a database it does
		through a [~BT~query~BT~ declaration](../queries.md): calling one answers with a
		~BT~hive.sql.Fragment<rows>~BT~ — the text, and the values bound beside it,
		nothing having run yet — and ~BT~run~BT~ is what runs one.

		SQL you genuinely assemble yourself goes through ~BT~raw~BT~, which is untyped by
		construction — a ~BT~Table~BT~, header row and all — and greppable by design.

		~CODE~hive
		proc main(): void \{
			opened := hive.sql.connect(hive.sql.DatabaseDriver.SQLite(), "./hive.db")
			if opened is Result.Error(why) \{
				echo "cannot open the database: \{why.message}"
				hive.term.exit(1)
			}
			if opened is Result.Ok(db) \{
				if hive.sql.run(db, findUser("ada")) is Result.Ok(rows) \{
					for each user in rows \{
						echo user.name
					}
				}
			}
		}
		~CODE~

		## A connection is a pool

		A ~BT~SqlConnection~BT~ is a **connection pool**, safe to hold for the life of the
		program and to share across virtual threads. **Open it once in ~BT~main~BT~ and pass
		it along; never open one per query.** A handler reaches it through a partial
		application — ~BT~hive.net.httpServe(8080, handle(db, _))~BT~.

		## The errors

		A ~BT~SqlError~BT~ is one of four variants, each carrying a ~BT~message~BT~:

		| variant | means |
		| --- | --- |
		| ~BT~Connection~BT~ | the database could not be opened or reached, or the connection is closed |
		| ~BT~Statement~BT~ | the statement itself failed |
		| ~BT~Shape~BT~ | a different number of columns came back |
		| ~BT~Convert~BT~ | a cell did not fit its field's type |

		## In-memory SQLite

		It belongs to the **connection**, not the process, which under a pool means each
		connection lands on a database of its own. Ask for a shared cache —
		~BT~pool(driver, "file::memory:?cache=shared", 8, 1)~BT~ — or pin the pool to one
		connection.

		A program that opens a database makes ~BT~hive container~BT~ resolve the drivers with
		~BT~go mod tidy~BT~ before it compiles; both drivers are pure Go, so nothing of them
		is left for the runtime stage to carry.
	`)
}

// --- hive.syslink ------------------------------------------------------------

func syslink(): Str {
	return page.backticked(`
		# ~BT~hive.syslink~BT~ — what a service is

		Addressable **services**, in this process or on another machine, reached by the
		same statement either way. A service is long-lived, owns private state only it
		can touch, and has an identity you can pass around: an ~BT~Address<M>~BT~, which
		says the type ~BT~M~BT~ of the messages it takes.

		A service is started, named and stopped by three **builtins** — ~BT~spawn~BT~,
		~BT~at~BT~ and ~BT~kill~BT~ — and what is left in the module is the node. Every call
		is [syslink-calls](syslink-calls.md); across machines is
		[syslink-nodes](syslink-nodes.md).

		## The handler is one turn

		~CODE~hive
		proc handler(state: mut State, message: Message): Message
		~CODE~

		A handler **writes its state in place and returns the answer**. The state is a
		~BT~mut~BT~ parameter only the service's own mailbox ever holds, and one message is
		handled at a time, so a handler's body is the only thing that ever touches that
		state and needs no lock to do it. A handler is an ordinary ~BT~proc~BT~, so a test can
		call it directly with a ~BT~mut~BT~ variable of its own.

		~CODE~hive
		type Op \{
			Add \{ word: Str }
			Count
			Total \{ n: Int }
		}

		proc counter(seen: mut Str[dyn], message: Op): Op \{
			if message is Op.Add(word) \{
				append(seen, word)
			}
			return Op.Total(len(seen))
		}

		proc main(): void \{
			Str[dyn] empty = []
			if spawn(counter, empty) is Result.Ok(cache) \{
				async cache(Op.Add("hello"))
				if cache(Op.Count()) is Result.Ok(reply) && reply is Op.Total(n) \{
					echo n
				}
			}
		}
		~CODE~

		The compiler holds a handler to that shape where the service is started: its
		first parameter is ~BT~mut~BT~, the state it starts with fits that parameter,
		and it returns its own mailbox type. ~BT~spawn~BT~ answers with a ~BT~Result~BT~, since
		a service started under a name may find the name taken, holding an
		~BT~Address<M>~BT~ read off the handler.

		## The mailbox type is the whole protocol

		**The reply type *is* the mailbox type**, so nothing is annotated: a service
		answers with one of its own messages. That is what makes one type declaration
		the whole contract between a caller and a service, on this machine or another —
		and the address carries it, so **a message of another type is a compile error**
		where it is sent. What the compiler cannot see is whether the service under a
		name really takes an ~BT~M~BT~, so a message that does not decode where it arrives
		fails as ~BT~Decode~BT~.

		**Every request is answered** with what its turn returned, and a caller on this
		node is handed its own copy, since the next turn may write the state the answer
		was read from. A fire-and-forget send discards it.

		## A crash is local to its service

		A ~BT~panic~BT~ inside a service body **kills only that service**: its monitors are
		told, its callers get ~BT~Down~BT~, and the node keeps running. **This is the one
		place ~BT~panic~BT~ does not stop the program.** The node says so on standard error,
		as ~BT~hive: service Counter crashed: <message>~BT~.

		## A service that knows its own address

		A handler may take a third parameter, an ~BT~Address<M>~BT~ of its own mailbox type,
		and is then handed its own address every turn — to give to work it starts, so that the result comes
		back as a message. ~BT~spawn~BT~ takes either shape:

		~CODE~hive
		proc fetcher(pending: mut Int, note: Note, me: Address<Note>): Note \{
			if note is Note.Fetch(url) \{
				pending += 1
				async download(me, url)
			} else if note is Note.Fetched(_) \{
				pending -= 1
			}
			return note
		}
		~CODE~

		## When to reach for one

		A program that needs **long-lived shared state** — a cache, a registry, a game
		world, a connection nobody else may touch. The language has no channel, no lock
		and no thread handle ([concurrency](../concurrency.md)); a service is
		what fills that gap, and a window is one too ([ui](ui.md)).
	`)
}

func syslinkCalls(): Str {
	return page.backticked(`
		# ~BT~hive.syslink~BT~ — the calls

		Three are **builtins**, reached with no module:

		| call | answers with |
		| --- | --- |
		| ~BT~spawn(handler, state)~BT~ | ~BT~Result<Address<M>, SyslinkError>~BT~ — starts a service |
		| ~BT~spawn(handler, state, name)~BT~ | the same, registered under ~BT~name~BT~ |
		| ~BT~at(name)~BT~ | ~BT~Address<M>~BT~ — that service on **this** node |
		| ~BT~at(endpoint, name)~BT~ | ~BT~Address<M>~BT~ — the same service on **another** |
		| ~BT~kill(address)~BT~ | ~BT~void~BT~ — stops it |

		The rest are the module's own, ~BT~hive.syslink.<call>~BT~:

		| call | answers with |
		| --- | --- |
		| ~BT~monitor(watcher, target, notice)~BT~ | ~BT~void~BT~ |
		| ~BT~listen(endpoint)~BT~ | ~BT~Result<Str, SyslinkError>~BT~ |
		| ~BT~node()~BT~ | ~BT~Str~BT~ |
		| ~BT~peers()~BT~ | ~BT~Str[dyn]~BT~ |
		| ~BT~setKey(key: Secret<Str>)~BT~ | ~BT~void~BT~ |

		~BT~Address<M>~BT~ is a builtin type, and **opaque**: an address is produced by ~BT~spawn~BT~
		or ~BT~at~BT~, and ~BT~Address~BT~ without its ~BT~M~BT~ is a compile error. A
		~BT~hive.syslink.SyslinkError~BT~ is a union of what went wrong, each variant carrying
		a ~BT~message~BT~:

		| variant | means |
		| --- | --- |
		| ~BT~Taken~BT~ | ~BT~spawn~BT~ was given a name another service holds |
		| ~BT~Down~BT~ | the service died before answering, or was already gone — at once |
		| ~BT~NoProc~BT~ | nothing is registered under that name on the node asked |
		| ~BT~Timeout~BT~ | no answer within the time given, or the default |
		| ~BT~Unreachable~BT~ | the node could not be reached |
		| ~BT~Decode~BT~ | the message did not decode as the type the service takes |
		| ~BT~NoPeer~BT~ | the address names no node to send to |
		| ~BT~NoListener~BT~ | ~BT~listen~BT~ could not listen where it was told, or this node has not listened and was asked to reach another |
		| ~BT~NoKey~BT~ | there is no cluster key to authenticate with |

		## An address is called

		There is exactly **one** way to reach a service, and — as with a func — the call
		site decides what it means:

		~CODE~hive
		async inbox(Note.Say("hi"))          // send it, wait for nothing; cannot fail
		answer := cache(Op.Count())          // send and wait: Result<Op, SyslinkError>
		answer := cache(Op.Count()) with timeout 250
		later  := async cache(Op.Count())    // wait where ~BT~later~BT~ is read
		both   := await [a(m), b(m)]         // one barrier, one deadline
		~CODE~

		A fire-and-forget send **cannot fail** and has nothing to report. A request to an
		~BT~Address<M>~BT~ carries an ~BT~M~BT~ and answers with ~BT~Result<M, SyslinkError>~BT~; a message
		of any other type is a compile error.

		~BT~with timeout~BT~ **folds into that same error** rather than wrapping a second
		~BT~Result~BT~ around the first, so the result is still ~BT~Result<M, SyslinkError>~BT~,
		failing as ~BT~SyslinkError.Timeout~BT~.

		## Names and addresses

		A service is named as it is **spawned**, by an **atom**, and a name is one
		service's at a time on a node: a second ~BT~spawn~BT~ under a name still held fails as
		~BT~Taken~BT~. One with no name has nothing to be refused, and is always ~BT~Ok~BT~.

		~BT~at~BT~ **performs no I/O and cannot fail** — it is address *construction*, not a
		lookup. Nothing is dialled until something is sent. A name says nothing about
		what the service takes, so **~BT~M~BT~ comes from where the address lands**, as
		~BT~hive.map.new()~BT~'s does: a declaration, or — bound with ~BT~:=~BT~ — the first later
		send, ~BT~x := at(#Cache)~BT~ then ~BT~x(Op.Get("k"))~BT~. Once settled it never changes.

		~CODE~hive
		if spawn(counter, empty, #Cache) is Result.Error(syslink.SyslinkError.Taken(_)) \{
			echo "somebody else is #Cache"
		}

		Address<Op> here  = at(#Cache)                    // this node
		Address<Op> there = at("10.0.0.4:9000", #Cache)   // another
		~CODE~

		**A named address is the only kind that survives its service being restarted**,
		which is the reason to name one at all: the address ~BT~spawn~BT~ answers with for a
		service with no name is that instance and nothing else.

		## Watching and killing

		~BT~monitor(watcher, target, notice)~BT~ posts ~BT~notice~BT~ to ~BT~watcher~BT~ — a service on
		this node, such as ~BT~at(#Supervisor)~BT~ or the address a handler is handed — when
		~BT~target~BT~ dies. The notice is one of the watcher's messages, and the target may
		take anything. That is how a supervisor is written, and it is why a crash
		being local is useful rather than merely quiet.

		~BT~kill(address)~BT~ ends a service, on this node or another. Its name is free
		again before its monitors hear of it, so a supervisor can spawn the next one
		under it straight away.
	`)
}

func syslinkNodes(): Str {
	return page.backticked(`
		# ~BT~hive.syslink~BT~ — across machines

		The same statement reaches a service in this process and one on another
		machine. What changes is only how its address was built:
		~BT~at(#Cache)~BT~ against ~BT~at("10.0.0.4:9000", #Cache)~BT~.

		| call | answers with |
		| --- | --- |
		| ~BT~listen(endpoint)~BT~ | ~BT~Result<Str, SyslinkError>~BT~ |
		| ~BT~node()~BT~ | ~BT~Str~BT~ — the endpoint this node answers on |
		| ~BT~peers()~BT~ | ~BT~Str[dyn]~BT~ — the endpoints it is connected to |
		| ~BT~setKey(key: Secret<Str>)~BT~ | ~BT~void~BT~ — this program's cluster key, from the next connection on |

		~CODE~hive
		if hive.syslink.listen("0.0.0.0:9000") is Result.Error(why) \{
			echo "cannot listen: \{why.message}"
			hive.term.exit(1)
		}
		echo "this node is \{hive.syslink.node()}"
		~CODE~

		**~BT~listen~BT~ answers with the endpoint a peer should dial.** A port of ~BT~0~BT~ asks the
		kernel to choose, and what comes back — and what ~BT~node()~BT~ says from then on —
		carries the port it chose. The host is left as written, and the port is bound on
		every interface — except a loopback host, which is bound as written.

		**A node has no name.** It is identified by the endpoint it can be dialled at,
		so a peer list is ordinary runtime data rather than a cluster configuration.
		[~BT~hive.net.localAddress()~BT~](net-names.md) is what a node prints so another can
		reach it.

		## On the wire

		* **One persistent, multiplexed connection per node *pair***, carrying
		  length-prefixed frames, **dialled lazily** — building an address opens nothing.
		* Messages cross as **JSON**, using the same derived codecs
		  [~BT~hive.json~BT~](json.md) builds — so a message type's ~BT~JSON as~BT~
		  annotations decide what goes on the wire here too. A mailbox type that JSON
		  cannot carry cannot travel: **a map does not**, and ~BT~toTable~BT~ is the
		  answer.
		* **Every frame carries a 32-bit structural digest of the message type**, so a
		  peer built from a different declaration **fails loudly instead of decoding
		  another type's bytes**. Two nodes must be built from the same mailbox
		  declaration. **A field goes into that digest under its JSON key**, since that
		  is what decides the bytes; a ~BT~Default~BT~ stays out, changing what a decoder
		  accepts rather than what an encoder writes.
		* **Every connection is TLS 1.3, mutually authenticated, with no plaintext
		  path.** There is nothing to turn on and nothing to turn off.

		## The cluster key

		What a connection proves both ends hold; two nodes with different keys turn
		each other away. It is the last ~BT~setKey~BT~ the program made, else
		~BT~HIVE_SYSLINK_KEY~BT~, else ~BT~~/.hive/syslink.key~BT~, created on first use.

		~CODE~hive
		if hive.env.getSecret("CLUSTER_KEY") is Result.Ok(key) \{
			hive.syslink.setKey(key)
		}
		~CODE~

		* **~BT~setKey~BT~ is the program's own**: held in memory, never written to
		  ~BT~syslink.key~BT~ and never put in the environment.
		* **It applies from the next connection on**, made or accepted; one already open
		  stays open.
		* ~BT~setKey(bypass(""))~BT~ panics: an empty key proves nothing.

		## Delivery is best-effort

		**Messages queued when a node is declared down are dropped.** A request gets an
		error and a fire-and-forget send gets nothing, so anything that must arrive is
		a request whose answer the caller checks — and ~BT~monitor~BT~ is how a service hears
		that something it depended on has gone.

		~BT~with timeout~BT~ is what bounds a call to a machine that may not answer, and it
		folds into ~BT~SyslinkError.Timeout~BT~:

		~CODE~hive
		Address<Op> far = at(endpoint, #Cache)
		if far(Op.Count()) with timeout 250 is Result.Error(syslink.SyslinkError.Timeout(_)) \{
			echo "no answer in time"
		}
		~CODE~
	`)
}

func audio(): Str {
	return page.backticked(`
		# ~BT~hive.audio~BT~

		Sound that is not in a scene: a recording or a **live stream** — an internet
		radio station, say — played by name until it is stopped.

		| call | answers |
		| --- | --- |
		| ~BT~play(name, url, volume)~BT~ | ~BT~void~BT~ |
		| ~BT~stop(name)~BT~ | ~BT~void~BT~ |

		~CODE~hive
		if inCar && radioOn \{
			hive.audio.play("radio", "https://example.com/live.mp3", 0.5)
		} else \{
			hive.audio.stop("radio")
		}
		~CODE~

		**The name is the stream's identity**, as a sound's is in a
		[scene](ui-scene-sound.md). ~BT~play~BT~ with a name already playing the same
		~BT~url~BT~ only sets its ~BT~volume~BT~ (0 to 1), so a program may say what it wants
		every frame and nothing restarts; a different ~BT~url~BT~ under the name replaces
		the stream. ~BT~stop~BT~ with a name that is not playing does nothing.

		**It is played by the program's window.** The window's engine is where the
		platform's decoders are — MP3, AAC, Ogg, whatever it reads — so a stream is
		buffered and decoded there, and picked up again a moment after it drops. Until
		a window is open nothing is heard, and a window opened later plays whatever is
		wanted by then. A window will not make a sound until somebody has touched it;
		a stream that was refused starts on the next key or tap.

		~BT~hive.audio~BT~ is a module of its own: it uses nothing of ~BT~hive.ui~BT~, and a
		program that never calls it carries none of it.
	`)
}
